         ],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### And Europe?\n\nIn Europe — probably independent of Asia — first the Greeks and then the Romans used a tool we know today as Calculi. It strongly resembles the Soroban (算盤) we learned about in Japan (Nippon-koku or Nihon-koku, 日本国).\n\nWorking with the Calculi is analogous to the Soroban (算盤), but we represent numbers in a different way:",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<div data-custom=\"abacus-calculi\"></div>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
         "next": "46a3664d-3f9e-4026-a8aa-0d5f7ebb4b81",
         "prev": "98eb71ca-3dff-4495-a5a1-50dd40cc1b11",
         "jumpTo": "",
         "answer": null,
         "score": 1,
         "position": ""
      },
      {
         "id": "46a3664d-3f9e-4026-a8aa-0d5f7ebb4b81",
         "audio": "",
         "smile": "party",
         "source_menu": [],
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "#### Conclusion\n\nIn this class, we learned about numerical tools created in Europe and Asia. Other continents created their own tools too; we left them out because they are not abacuses like the ones we studied.\n\nIn the next classes, we are going to learn how to use these abacuses.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ],
         "desc": null,
